(796613, 84692, 8522, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Strange looking photo, I''m presuming this is a model, although you haven''t left any comments for me to go by.<br/>As a photo in it''s own right, I''m not really sure what you wanted to achieve with this shot... the use of light doesn''t really inspire me here, and there is no clear point of focus. The composition seems a little haphazard. Without a photographer''s comment, I''m not sure I can say a lot more about this photo!<br/> <br/>Bob</td>', 'Critique Club: Strange looking photo, I''m presuming this is a model, although you haven''t left any comments for me to go by.As a photo in it''s own right, I''m not really sure what you wanted to achieve with this shot... the use of light doesn''t really inspire me here, and there is no clear point of focus. The composition seems a little haphazard. Without a photographer''s comment, I''m not sure I can say a lot more about this photo! Bob', '2004-07-05 14:44:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(796918, 85031, 7743, '<td valign="top">*** Critique Club ***<br/> <br/>The colour and the exposure on the flag are good, however I get the feeling there is something missing.  It could be the fact that the entire flag isn''t visible or the fact that the person with their arm raised doesn''t have much visible detail.  <br/> <br/>More details in the photo to identify with England I believe would make this a better image and portray the emotion that your title implies better.<br/> <br/>Good choice of topic for this challenge.<br/> <br/>Keep shooting.<br/> <br/>Colette</td>', '*** Critique Club *** The colour and the exposure on the flag are good, however I get the feeling there is something missing.  It could be the fact that the entire flag isn''t visible or the fact that the person with their arm raised doesn''t have much visible detail.   More details in the photo to identify with England I believe would make this a better image and portray the emotion that your title implies better. Good choice of topic for this challenge. Keep shooting. Colette', '2004-07-05 19:09:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(797059, 84679, 22734, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/>  Okay, this is a sports action shot and as such we are at the mercy of the available lightand of course the swift movement. The image has pleasant colors, but contrast needs a boost. The image is not in dead focus.<br/>  Of course, the dynamics would change in your favor had you waited to catch him at the end of the swing. This would have added more excitement. As a matter of fact many sport photographers shoot this on drive mode. <br/>  Another thing you can do with this picture as is, is to crop it like a baseball card.<br/>  The one lesson you should learn from this effort, is to resolve the focusing aspect. Learn the knack of prefocusing and then waiting for the action, but if much movement takes place do not hesitate to refocus.<br/>  By looking at your port I can see that you are well capable of focusing your camera, so all you need is a couple more visits to the arena. You will learn that a different mind set is required for different situations.</td>', 'From the Critique Club:  Okay, this is a sports action shot and as such we are at the mercy of the available lightand of course the swift movement. The image has pleasant colors, but contrast needs a boost. The image is not in dead focus.  Of course, the dynamics would change in your favor had you waited to catch him at the end of the swing. This would have added more excitement. As a matter of fact many sport photographers shoot this on drive mode.   Another thing you can do with this picture as is, is to crop it like a baseball card.  The one lesson you should learn from this effort, is to resolve the focusing aspect. Learn the knack of prefocusing and then waiting for the action, but if much movement takes place do not hesitate to refocus.  By looking at your port I can see that you are well capable of focusing your camera, so all you need is a couple more visits to the arena. You will learn that a different mind set is required for different situations.', '2004-07-05 21:51:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(797083, 84735, 22734, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/>  First: this is a beautiful action shot. The composition is fairly nice as is. Arguments could be made about the crop, but you captured the start and the end of the cloud. To bring the plabe closer would mess up your resolution. The way it is it gives the viewer a majestic and vast sense of the subject. All in all, I believe you should have gotten a higher score, after all, the main objective is the quality of the photography.<br/>  Well, not so. When you enter a challange, mainly the photographers will pin you against the wall if you image is good but it is misnamed. I am certain outside voters could not really care because they have no stake in the pretext under which the picture is taken. They just want to see exciting and colorful photography.<br/>  You should then consider giving as much care to the title, in this case a headline, as you do to the picture. However, must of us are not copywriter and we all fail miserably at times. I did so myself because I chose a famous playland that was being demolished and I used the headline, "Demolition of Old memories." Of course, if you lived near Asbury Park the news was readilly understood. But outside of this geography it is meaningless.<br/>  So keep up the good work and give the spirit of the challenge more attention. dan  Hey: I can take this advise myself.</td>', 'From the Critique Club:  First: this is a beautiful action shot. The composition is fairly nice as is. Arguments could be made about the crop, but you captured the start and the end of the cloud. To bring the plabe closer would mess up your resolution. The way it is it gives the viewer a majestic and vast sense of the subject. All in all, I believe you should have gotten a higher score, after all, the main objective is the quality of the photography.  Well, not so. When you enter a challange, mainly the photographers will pin you against the wall if you image is good but it is misnamed. I am certain outside voters could not really care because they have no stake in the pretext under which the picture is taken. They just want to see exciting and colorful photography.  You should then consider giving as much care to the title, in this case a headline, as you do to the picture. However, must of us are not copywriter and we all fail miserably at times. I did so myself because I chose a famous playland that was being demolished and I used the headline, "Demolition of Old memories." Of course, if you lived near Asbury Park the news was readilly understood. But outside of this geography it is meaningless.  So keep up the good work and give the spirit of the challenge more attention. dan  Hey: I can take this advise myself.', '2004-07-05 22:17:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(797099, 84786, 12611, '<td valign="top">nice shot and title!!!<br/>but it appears from your picture- and I can attest to this- that justice has no brains!<br/> <br/>ques.     what do you call a lawyer with a 72 IQ?<br/> <br/>ans.      your honor.</td>', 'nice shot and title!!!but it appears from your picture- and I can attest to this- that justice has no brains! ques.     what do you call a lawyer with a 72 IQ? ans.      your honor.', '2004-07-05 22:35:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(797110, 85275, 12611, '<td valign="top">this is a great shot. the grain, the contrast, the way the people are moving... you should have won this challenge- problem is voters appear to be searching for large - fill th eframe subject matter.<br/>I like this shot though- good going.</td>', 'this is a great shot. the grain, the contrast, the way the people are moving... you should have won this challenge- problem is voters appear to be searching for large - fill th eframe subject matter.I like this shot though- good going.', '2004-07-05 22:41:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(797212, 84723, 17492, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critque Club!<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/>This is a stellar "newspaper" photo. Your photo really demonstrates the problems large cities have to deal with. Just a personal suggestion, I would''ve loved to see some exhaust coming out of the back of the bus as it pulled away from the curb. It would''ve really been a nice touch. A second suggestion would''ve been to get a "rush hour" look with the smog. Since public transportation is actually a way to decrease smog, not increase it. Not to say that''s what you were trying to portray, but simply what would''ve added to this shot.<br/> <br/>Lighting:<br/>Your lighting is a little dark, chances are the camera over-compensated for the light smog at the top of the frame. If you can do exposure compensation on your photo, that would''ve helped it. I still like it how it is though.<br/> <br/>Technical:<br/>Your technique is sound. The over-exposing a hair would be the only thing I can recommend as that is concerned.<br/> <br/>Post-Processing<br/>The photo is in focus if only a little soft from the resize. Unsharp mask after resizing would''ve sharpened up your photo. (the bottom close stuff specifically)<br/> <br/>You might also want to consider using a perspective adjustment. Since your wide angle makes the buildings lean toward each other. Getting them straight would be slightly more pleasing to the eye.<br/> <br/>Overall:<br/>I like this shot a lot. You captured this challenge spot on. With a little adjustment to your exposure, and minor editing I think you could''ve placed higher.<br/> <br/>I hope you do better in the future. Keep up the excellent submissions.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critque Club! Composition:This is a stellar "newspaper" photo. Your photo really demonstrates the problems large cities have to deal with. Just a personal suggestion, I would''ve loved to see some exhaust coming out of the back of the bus as it pulled away from the curb. It would''ve really been a nice touch. A second suggestion would''ve been to get a "rush hour" look with the smog. Since public transportation is actually a way to decrease smog, not increase it. Not to say that''s what you were trying to portray, but simply what would''ve added to this shot. Lighting:Your lighting is a little dark, chances are the camera over-compensated for the light smog at the top of the frame. If you can do exposure compensation on your photo, that would''ve helped it. I still like it how it is though. Technical:Your technique is sound. The over-exposing a hair would be the only thing I can recommend as that is concerned. Post-ProcessingThe photo is in focus if only a little soft from the resize. Unsharp mask after resizing would''ve sharpened up your photo. (the bottom close stuff specifically) You might also want to consider using a perspective adjustment. Since your wide angle makes the buildings lean toward each other. Getting them straight would be slightly more pleasing to the eye. Overall:I like this shot a lot. You captured this challenge spot on. With a little adjustment to your exposure, and minor editing I think you could''ve placed higher. I hope you do better in the future. Keep up the excellent submissions.', '2004-07-06 00:10:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(797537, 85170, 19871, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by KaDi:</b></div><hr/><i> Well conceived subject.  I believe you would need higher contrast for most newsprint, however. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Yes, I agree but that side would be down to the newspaper editor/art director....<br/> <br/>I''ve a submission into a local newspaper which I''m hoping will get printed on weds/thurs. If so, it will make a nice tear sheet :-)</td>', 'Originally posted by KaDi: Well conceived subject.  I believe you would need higher contrast for most newsprint, however.   Yes, I agree but that side would be down to the newspaper editor/art director.... I''ve a submission into a local newspaper which I''m hoping will get printed on weds/thurs. If so, it will make a nice tear sheet :-)', '2004-07-06 10:06:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(797539, 85170, 19871, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by frogs:</b></div><hr/><i> Great capture, one of the few images I have seen that actually could be in a newspaper. Personally I think I would have stayed in colour for this to portray the present a little more. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I did debate with myself over keeping this in colour, but apart from the major papers most still print in BnW and this one although "a snap shot," seemed to fit the chalenge and I felt was similar to what a local press photographer would take.<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by frogs: Great capture, one of the few images I have seen that actually could be in a newspaper. Personally I think I would have stayed in colour for this to portray the present a little more.   I did debate with myself over keeping this in colour, but apart from the major papers most still print in BnW and this one although "a snap shot," seemed to fit the chalenge and I felt was similar to what a local press photographer would take.', '2004-07-06 10:09:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(797547, 85170, 19871, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by NicNic101:</b></div><hr/><i> this is a cool shot.  flowers? ....  interesting .... what state is this in? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Ilkeston, near nottingham in the UK.<br/> <br/>For some reason its become quite a tradition to lay flowers close to a road death... I think it may have started around princes Di''s death as I dont recall it being in favour prior to that, or not as regular.<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by NicNic101: this is a cool shot.  flowers? ....  interesting .... what state is this in?   Ilkeston, near nottingham in the UK. For some reason its become quite a tradition to lay flowers close to a road death... I think it may have started around princes Di''s death as I dont recall it being in favour prior to that, or not as regular.', '2004-07-06 10:11:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(797548, 85170, 19871, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by jadin:</b></div><hr/><i> Good photo. (headlines usually aren''t questions - me being picky) Good attempt. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>You may be correct there... most papers do tend to have the answer they feel is correct, then word the title accordingly... perhaps "Speed cameras fail to reduce deaths" with the subsiquent story trying to prove that.<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by jadin: Good photo. (headlines usually aren''t questions - me being picky) Good attempt.   You may be correct there... most papers do tend to have the answer they feel is correct, then word the title accordingly... perhaps "Speed cameras fail to reduce deaths" with the subsiquent story trying to prove that.', '2004-07-06 10:14:44.000000', NULL, 0),
